  "title": "Doubling every lag keeps every stage balanced; slower doubling does not",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "For running networks of rule-60 rings with law (12,12) contacts read as one space-time law: if at a lag no cell outside a ring X holds a part of X's born cells and X's own cells hold them independently, every contact binds X from then on exactly as in the static stage. Under the doubling law at every lag this holds for every ring at every lag (isolation, and a unipotent evolution of each ring's own cells), so every stage, a square torus with sides at least 3, is balanced at every lag. Computed: doubling every 2, 4, 6 or 8 lags from the 3 x 3 tube at g = 2 breaks isolation from the second doubling and leaves a stage unbalanced by 24 x 24; every 3 lags keeps both, to 48 x 48.",
